/// Chapter 8 of the guide, written from md-docs/learn/08-events.md by
/// scripts/docsite/guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page Learn08Events(path: "/learn/08-events", title: "Events", description: "DOM event handlers, the event object, keyboard shortcuts, component events, modals, menus, popovers, gestures and window events.", layout: DocsShell("Events", kind: .tutorial, chapter: t("nav.group.language"), blurb: "Handlers on elements, keyboard shortcuts, the events components fire, the overlays a page opens, and drag and drop.", source: "md-docs/learn/08-events.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "handlers", label: "Handlers"), Anchor(id: "keys", label: "Keys"), Anchor(id: "emitting-events-from-components", label: "Emitting events from components"), Anchor(id: "overlays-modals-dialogs-toasts-and-menus", label: "Overlays: modals, dialogs, toasts and menus"), Anchor(id: "gestures-drag-long-press-swipe-and-the-splitter", label: "Gestures: drag, long-press, swipe and the splitter"), Anchor(id: "listening-outside-the-page-s-elements", label: "Listening outside the page's elements"), Anchor(id: "summary", label: "Summary"), Anchor(id: "common-mistakes", label: "Common mistakes")], learned: ["Handle a DOM event with `on click \{ \}`, and read the event with `on keydown(e) \{ \}`", "Answer a key or a chord with `on key(\"mod+k\")`, on an element or for the whole app", "Fire your component's own events with `event` and `emit`", "Open modals, menus, popovers and tooltips from state, and know how they stack", "Drag, drop, long-press, swipe and listen to the window"], prevTo: "/learn/07-state-and-reactivity", prevLabel: t("ch.07"), nextTo: "/learn/09-control-flow", nextLabel: t("ch.09"))) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("This chapter covers events in WebFluent. An event handler is the code that runs when something happens: a click, a key, a drop, a component saying it changed. You write it as `on <event> \{ … \}` inside the block of the element it listens to, and its body can change state, call actions and navigate.\n\nIn this chapter:\n\n- handlers and the event object\n- keys, and shortcuts for a whole app\n- the events your own components fire\n- overlays: modals, sheets, toasts, menus, popovers and tooltips, what closes them, and how they stack\n- gestures: drag and drop, long-press, swipe and the splitter\n- listening to the window and the document")

        Heading("Handlers", id: "handlers").h2

        Markdown("A handler is written inside an element's block: `on <event> \{ … \}`, or `on <event>(name) \{ … \}` when the body reads the event object. This page counts clicks and logs what happens in a text field:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Clicks(path: "/", title: "Clicks", description: "Handlers on a button and a field.") {
    state n = 0
    state last = ""
    Heading("Clicks").h1
    Button("+1") { on click { n = n + 1 } }
    Input(bind: last, placeholder: "Type here") {
        on input { log(last) }
        on keydown(e) { if e.key == "Enter" { log("submitted {last}") } }
        on focus { log("in") }
        on blur { log("out") }
    }
    Text("{n}")
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("A handler may name any DOM event. The common ones are `click`, `input`, `change`, `submit`, `focus`, `blur`, `keydown`, `keyup`, `mouseenter` and `mouseleave`. The rest — `dblclick`, `contextmenu`, `pointerdown`, `pointermove`, `touchstart`, `wheel`, `scroll`, `paste`, `drop`, `dragover`, `animationend`, `ended` and the others — work the same way. The [components reference](/reference#on-every-element) lists every one. On a call of your own component, the events it declares ([Components](/learn/10-components#events)) join them.\n\nA handler's body is an imperative block, so it may assign state, call actions, `await` and `navigate`.")

        Callout("**Coming from React or Svelte?** A handler is not a prop: there is no `onClick=\{…\}` and no `on:click=\{…\}`. Write `on click \{ … \}` inside the element's block. `onclick:` as an argument is an inline handler, and the compiler refuses it (`E106`). See [Coming from React](/coming-from/react#the-map).", kind: .note)

        Markdown("A handler on a component call — `UserCard(\"Sam\") \{ on click \{ … \} \}` — attaches to the component's root element, so a styled button component is clickable wherever it is used.")

        Heading("The event object", id: "the-event-object").h3

        Markdown("To read the event, name a parameter: `on keydown(e) \{ … \}`. It is the browser's own event object, so `e.key`, `e.target.value`, `e.clientX`, `e.preventDefault()` and `e.stopPropagation()` are all there.\n\nWithout a parameter, the event is still in scope as `event` — unless the program has an `event` of its own (a loop's item, a state, an action), which wins. Name the parameter to read the event there.\n\nA DOM event sends one value, the event, so a handler names one name at most: `on click(e, x)` is an error (`T22`). Read what you want from the event, `e.clientX`. Several names are for a component's own events, which send what they declare ([below](#emitting-events-from-components)).\n\nThis drop zone uses the event object twice — to allow the drop, and to read the dropped files:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Drop(path: "/", title: "Drop", description: "A place to drop files.") {
    state names: [String] = []
    Heading("Drop files here").h1
    Card {
        on dragover(e) { e.preventDefault() }
        on drop(e) {
            e.preventDefault()
            names = Array.from(e.dataTransfer.files).map(f => f.name)
        }
        Text(if names.length == 0 { "Nothing yet" } else { names.join(", ") })
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Heading("How often a handler runs", id: "how-often-a-handler-runs").h3

        Markdown("An event can come far more often than its handler needs to run: a key per letter, a scroll per pixel. Say how often in the handler's parentheses:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Reader(path: "/", title: "Reader", description: "A search box and a long page.") {
    state typed = ""
    state q = ""
    state read = 0
    state loads = 0
    Heading("Reader").h1
    Input(bind: typed, label: "Search") {
        on input(debounce: 300.ms) { q = typed }
    }
    Stack {
        style { height: 300px; overflow-y: auto }
        on scroll(e, throttle: 100.ms) { read = e.target.scrollTop }
        Stack { style { height: 2000px; flex-shrink: 0 } Text("A long article") }
    }
    Text("Searching for {q}, scrolled {read}px")
    Text("End of the list") { on visible { loads = loads + 1 } }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("- **`debounce: 300.ms`** runs the handler once the events have stopped coming for that long, with the last of them: a search asked when the reader stops typing.\n- **`throttle: 100.ms`** runs it at once, then at most once in that long, and once more where the events stopped: a scroll position followed without running on every pixel.\n- A wait still pending when the page, branch or item leaves is dropped.\n- **`on visible`** runs each time the element comes into view: scrolled to, or drawn where the reader is looking. A \"load more\" row is an element with `on visible \{ feed.loadMore() \}` at the end of the list.")

        Heading("Keys", id: "keys").h2

        Markdown("`on key(\"…\")` answers to one key, with modifiers. On an element it fires while that element has focus; at the top of a page or component it fires anywhere on the page:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Keys(path: "/", title: "Keys", description: "Keys on a field and on the page.") {
    state open = false
    state q = ""
    Heading("Keys").h1
    Input(bind: q, ref: box, placeholder: "Search") {
        on key("Escape") { q = "" }            // on the element: while it has focus
        on key("Enter", e) { log(e.target.value) }
    }
    on key("mod+k") { box.focus() }            // on the page: anywhere on the page
    on key("?") { open = !open }
    if open { Alert("Shortcuts: {keyLabel("mod+k")} to search, ? for this.").info }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("How a combination is spelled:\n\n- Modifiers are `mod`, `ctrl`, `shift`, `alt` and `meta` (or `cmd`), joined with `+`. `mod` is ⌘ on a Mac and Ctrl everywhere else, so one shortcut works on both; write `ctrl` or `cmd` only when you mean exactly that key.\n- Then comes the key as the browser names it: `k`, `Enter`, `Escape` (or `esc`), `Tab`, `ArrowDown` (or `down`), `space`, `F2`.\n- `on key(\"Enter\", e)` names the event too.\n\nHow it listens:\n\n- On an element, the handler listens on that element.\n- At the top of a page or component, it listens on the document for as long as the page shows.\n- Every modifier must match exactly, so `ctrl+k` does not fire on `ctrl+shift+k`.\n\nA combination no keyboard sends — an unknown modifier, no key — is an error (`E117`).")

        Heading("Shortcuts for an app", id: "shortcuts-for-an-app").h3

        Markdown("To give an app shortcuts that work on every page, write them with `mod` and declare them on `app`:\n\n- **`mod`** is ⌘ on a Mac and Ctrl everywhere else.\n- On **`app`**, a shortcut is heard on every page for as long as the app runs.\n- A **chord** is its steps with a space between: `mod+k mod+s`. The second step must come within a second and a half of the first, and on its own it does not fire a shortcut of its own.\n\nThis app drives its shortcuts and its **View** menu from one table of commands:")

        CodeBlock(#"store Commands {
    state said = ""
    state table: [Command] = [
        { id: "palette", keys: "mod+shift+p", title: "Commands" },
        { id: "sidebar", keys: "mod+b", title: "Toggle sidebar" },
    ]
    action run(e) {
        for c in table {
            if keyIs(e, c.keys) {
                e.preventDefault()
                said = c.id
            }
        }
    }
}

type Command { id: String, keys: String, title: String }

app {
    on key("mod+s") { Commands.said = "saved" }
    on key("mod+k mod+s") { Commands.said = "shortcut help" }
    on keydown(e) { Commands.run(e) }
    Menu(trigger: "View") {
        for c in Commands.table {
            Menu.Item(shortcut: c.keys) { Text(c.title) }
        }
    }
    Text("Commands: {keyLabel("mod+shift+p")}")
    Router
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The helpers and rules it relies on:\n\n- **`keyIs(e, \"mod+s\")`** is whether a key event is that combination — a chord's too — so one table of commands can drive the shortcuts, the menus and a palette.\n- **`keyLabel(\"mod+shift+p\")`** is how this system writes it: `⇧⌘P` on a Mac, `Ctrl+Shift+P` elsewhere. A `Menu.Item`'s and a `Tooltip`'s `shortcut:` are written the same way, and the item — or the tooltip's control — tells assistive technology with `aria-keyshortcuts`.\n- **Events outside any element.** A page, a component or `app` may handle `keydown` and `keyup`, and the window's `resize`, `scroll`, `hashchange`, `popstate`, `online`, `offline`, `message`, `storage` and `visibilitychange`, and the window losing and gaining focus (`blur`, `focus` — an editor saves on leaving, for example). Each listens for as long as its owner shows.\n- **A letter is also the key in its place.** On a Mac `⌥⌘P` sends `π`, and `mod+alt+p` still matches it.")

        Heading("Moving through a group with the arrow keys", id: "moving-through-a-group-with-the-arrow-keys").h3

        Markdown("A tab strip, a toolbar or a row of choices is one stop for Tab: Tab enters it and leaves it, and the arrow keys move inside it. `Tabs` and `Menu` work that way already. `roving:` gives any element of yours the same:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Editor(path: "/", title: "Editor", description: "Format the text.") {
    state bold = false
    state italic = false
    Heading("Editor").h1
    Row(roving: .x, role: "toolbar", aria-label: "Format") {
        Button("Bold", aria-pressed: bold) { on click { bold = !bold } }
        Button("Italic", aria-pressed: italic) { on click { italic = !italic } }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("- `roving: .x` moves with ArrowLeft and ArrowRight, `.y` with ArrowUp and ArrowDown, `.both` with either.\n- Across a right-to-left page the next item is to the left, so ArrowLeft moves forward there.\n- Home and End go to the ends, and a letter goes to the next item whose name starts with it.\n- Tab enters at the chosen item, the one marked `aria-selected`, `aria-checked` or `aria-pressed`, or the first. A disabled item is passed over, and a key held with Alt, Ctrl or ⌘ is left to the browser.\n\nFor a key of your own that follows the direction of the text, write `on key(\"inline-start\")` or `on key(\"inline-end\")`: ArrowLeft and ArrowRight in a left-to-right box, the other way round in a right-to-left one.")

        Heading("Emitting events from components", id: "emitting-events-from-components").h2

        Markdown("A component declares the events it fires with `event`, and fires them with `emit`. The caller handles them like DOM events, with a name for each value the event sends — `on change(v)`, `on menu(key, x, y)`. [Components](/learn/10-components#events) covers the details. This counter tells its page every time it changes:")

        CodeBlock(#"component Counter(_ start: Number) {
    event change(value: Number)
    state n = start
    Row(gap: .sm) {
        Button("−") { on click { n = n - 1  emit change(n) } }
        Text("{n}")
        Button("+") { on click { n = n + 1  emit change(n) } }
    }
}

page Tally(path: "/", title: "Tally", description: "A component that reports its count.") {
    state total = 0
    Heading("Tally").h1
    Counter(10) { on change(v) { total = v } }
    Text("Now {total}")
}"#, title: "wf")

        Heading("Overlays: modals, dialogs, toasts and menus", id: "overlays-modals-dialogs-toasts-and-menus").h2

        Markdown("An *overlay* is something a page opens over itself: a modal that asks a question, a toast that confirms, a menu of actions, a tooltip. Most are opened by a state, so the page decides when they show. This page has one of each:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Overlays(path: "/", title: "Overlays", description: "What a page opens.") {
    state confirm = false
    state deleted = false
    Heading("Overlays").h1
    Button("Delete").danger { on click { confirm = true } }
    Modal(visible: confirm, title: "Delete this?") {
        Text("This cannot be undone.")
        Modal.Footer {
            Button("Cancel") { on click { confirm = false } }
            Button("Delete").danger { on click { confirm = false  deleted = true } }
        }
    }
    if deleted { Toast("Deleted").success }
    Menu(trigger: "More") {
        Menu.Item { Text("Rename") }
        Menu.Divider
        Menu.Item { Text("Archive") }
    }
    Tooltip("Copies the link") { IconButton(icon: "copy", label: "Copy link") }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("- **`Modal`** and **`Dialog`** are real `<dialog>` elements, open while their `visible:` state is true.\n  - The browser traps focus and makes the rest of the page inert. Escape or a press on the backdrop closes it, and the state is set back to `false`, so it never disagrees with what is on screen.\n  - When it opens, the focus goes to what says `autofocus`, else its first control; when it closes, the focus goes back to what had it.\n  - It fades out before it goes, however it was closed.\n  - `on close` runs when it closes, however it was closed.\n  - `title:` is its heading, which names it (`A08` without one). `Modal.Footer` is the row of actions, and `.closable` adds a close button. [`Modal`](/reference#modal) lists the rest.\n- **`Toast`** shows the moment it is rendered and leaves after three seconds. It is announced politely to screen readers. Put it under a condition that turns true, or call `toast(…)` from a handler (below).\n- **`Menu`** and **`Dropdown`** open a list from a button. Arrow keys move between items, Home and End jump to the ends, a letter jumps to the next item that starts with it, Enter and Space choose, Escape closes with focus back on the button, and a click outside closes it.\n- **`Tooltip`** shows its text when its child is hovered or focused, and is linked to it with `aria-describedby`.")

        Heading("What closes an overlay, and where the focus goes", id: "what-closes-an-overlay-and-where-the-focus-goes").h3

        Markdown("`dismiss:` says what closes a `Modal`, a `Dialog`, a `Sheet` or a `Popover` besides the page's own button: `.any` (Escape or a press outside, the default), `.escape`, or `.none`, for a form that must be answered. `initialFocus:` names, by its `ref:`, what takes the focus when it opens:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Profile(path: "/", title: "Profile", description: "A modal that starts in a field.") {
    state editing = false
    state name = ""
    Heading("Profile").h1
    Button("Edit profile") { on click { editing = true } }
    Modal(visible: editing, title: "Edit profile", dismiss: .escape, initialFocus: nameField) {
        Input(bind: name, label: "Name", ref: nameField)
        Modal.Footer { Button("Save").primary { on click { editing = false } } }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("An **`AlertDialog`** asks before something that cannot be undone. A screen reader hears an alert dialog, read with its body; Escape answers it, and a press beside it does not. Put the answer that loses nothing first in its footer.\n\nA **`Sheet`** is a modal that comes in from an edge: `side: .bottom` (the default), `.start` or `.end`, which swap in a right-to-left page. Dragged by its handle or heading toward that edge, past 30% of its size, it closes; a bottom sheet with `snap:` points opens at the first and settles at the nearest one it is let go near:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Shop(path: "/", title: "Shop", description: "Filters in a bottom sheet.") {
    state filters = false
    state sort = "new"
    Heading("Shop").h1
    Button("Filters") { on click { filters = true } }
    Sheet(visible: filters, title: "Filters", snap: [0.5, 1]).closable {
        Radio(bind: sort, value: "new", label: "Newest")
        Radio(bind: sort, value: "price", label: "Price")
        Sheet.Footer { Button("Show results").primary { on click { filters = false } } }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("On a narrow screen a `Sidebar` opens over the page as a drawer, which is a layer like these: the focus goes in and stays there, Escape or a press outside closes it, and the focus goes back to its toggle.")

        Heading("Toasts, questions and announcements from a handler", id: "toasts-questions-and-announcements-from-a-handler").h3

        Markdown("Three functions open an overlay from code rather than from state:")

        CodeBlock(#"store Mail {
    state archived: [String] = []
    action archive(id: String) {
        archived = archived.concat([id])
        toast("Conversation archived", action: "Undo", onAction: restore)
    }
    action restore() { archived = archived.slice(0, -1) }
}

page Inbox(path: "/", title: "Inbox", description: "Toasts, a question, an announcement.") {
    use Mail
    state gone = false
    Heading("Inbox").h1
    Button("Archive") { on click { Mail.archive("m1") } }
    Button("Delete everything").danger {
        on click {
            if await ask("Delete every message?", detail: "This cannot be undone.", yes: "Delete", danger: true) {
                gone = true
                announce("Every message deleted", .assertive)
            }
        }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("- **`toast(message, …)`** shows a toast above everything, a modal included, and says it to a screen reader. It takes `tone:` (`.success`, `.danger`, `.warning`, `.info`), `duration:` in milliseconds, and `action:` with `onAction:` for a button on it. It waits while the pointer is on it or the focus is in it, has a close button, and no more than three show at once. `toast.promise(p, loading: …, done: …, failed: …)` follows a promise.\n- **`await ask(question, …)`** draws an alert dialog and is `true` for yes, `false` for no or Escape. It takes `detail:`, `yes:`, `no:` and `danger: true`; the focus starts on no. (`confirm` is the browser's.)\n- **`announce(text)`** says something to a screen reader with nothing on screen moving; `announce(text, .assertive)` interrupts, for what cannot wait.\n\nA program that names something `toast`, `ask` or `announce` of its own keeps it; the function is then out of reach there.")

        Heading("Layers: popovers, menus and tooltips above everything", id: "layers-popovers-menus-and-tooltips-above-everything").h3

        Markdown("A `Popover`, a `Menu`'s list and a `Tooltip` are drawn in the browser's *top layer*, above everything else on the page, so a box with `overflow: hidden` cannot clip them. Each is placed against its *anchor*, the element it belongs to, and flips or slides to stay on screen.\n\nThis page opens a popover at a button and a context menu at the pointer:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Editor(path: "/", title: "Editor", description: "Layers at a control and at the pointer.") {
    state branches = false
    state asked = false
    state point = { x: 0, y: 0 }
    state said = ""
    Heading("Editor").h1
    Button("main", ref: branch) { on click { branches = !branches } }
    Popover(visible: branches, anchor: branch, label: "Branches") {
        on close(reason) { said = "closed by {reason}" }
        Text("main")
        Text("feature/layers")
    }
    Card {
        on contextmenu(e) {
            e.preventDefault()
            point = { x: e.clientX, y: e.clientY }
            asked = true
        }
        Text("Right-click for the context menu")
    }
    Menu(visible: asked, at: point) {
        Menu.Item(shortcut: "F2") { Text("Rename") }
        Menu.Divider
        Menu.Item.danger { Text("Delete") }
    }
    Text(said)
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("- **`Popover`** is open while its `visible:` state is true, placed against `anchor:`, an element's `ref:`. Escape or a press outside closes it (as `dismiss:` allows), sets `visible:` back to `false`, and `on close(reason)` hears `\"escape\"` or `\"outside\"`. Its anchor says what it opens and whether it is open (`aria-controls`, `aria-expanded`), the focus goes to its first control, and `label:` is its name (`A18` without one).\n- **`Menu`** without `trigger:` is a list that state opens, at an `anchor:` or `at:` a point. Choosing an item closes it.\n- The open layers form one stack, newest on top. Escape closes the newest layer that can be dismissed — a popover, a menu, an open `Modal` or `Dialog` — and nothing behind it hears that key. A modal opened from a popover takes the first Escape; the popover takes the next.\n- A tooltip keeps Escape only while focus is on its control. Otherwise the key goes on to the layer below, or to the element with focus.\n- A layer takes the attributes and handlers any element takes — `role:`, `aria-*`, `ref:` — on the element it draws. A `Popover` is a `dialog` unless its `role:` says otherwise.\n\nEvery option of these — `placement:`, `offset:`, `.arrow`, a menu's `checked:`, `disabled:` and `Menu.Sub`, a tooltip's `delay:` and `shortcut:` — is listed under [`Popover`](/reference#popover), [`Menu`](/reference#menu) and [`Tooltip`](/reference#tooltip). To ask before a destructive action, see [Build a confirm dialog](/recipes/confirm-dialog).")

        Heading("Gestures: drag, long-press, swipe and the splitter", id: "gestures-drag-long-press-swipe-and-the-splitter").h2

        Markdown("The runtime turns what the pointer does over time into one event that says what it means. A mouse, a pen and a finger run the same code. A finger picks a drag up with a long press, so a plain swipe still scrolls the page.\n\nThis list reorders its items by drag and drop:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Tabs(path: "/", title: "Tabs", description: "Drag to reorder.") {
    state tabs = ["App.wf", "Home.wf", "Shell.wf"]

    action move(what: String, near: String, position: String) {
        let rest = tabs.filter((x) => x != what)
        let at = rest.indexOf(near)
        let i = if position == "after" { at + 1 } else { at }
        tabs = [...rest.slice(0, i), what, ...rest.slice(i)]
    }

    Heading("Tabs").h1
    Row {
        for tab in tabs by tab {
            Card(drag: tab, dragGroup: "tabs", dropAxis: .x) {
                on receive(d) { move(d.value, tab, d.position) }
                Text(tab)
            }
        }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("- **`drag: value`** picks an element up: a mouse moved a few pixels, or a finger held still. Escape puts it back, and the click a drag ends with is not a click.\n- **`on receive(d)`** takes a drop: `d.value` is what was dropped, and `d.position` is `\"before\"` or `\"after\"` along `dropAxis:` (`.y`, down the page, unless you say `.x`), or `\"inside\"` on a target with `dropInside: true`.\n- **`dragGroup:`** on a drag and its targets keeps groups apart, such as tabs and tree rows.\n- **`on longpress(p)`** is half a second held still; **`on swipe(s)`** is a finger or a pen swept across, with `s.direction`.\n- **`Splitter(bind: side, min: 160, max: 480, reset: 240, label: \"Sidebar width\")`** sizes the region beside it, by drag or by the arrow keys. A drag or a key starts from the region's size as it is drawn.\n\n[On every element](/reference#on-every-element) lists the drag props and the gesture events in full, `dropValue:` and `on dragmove(d)` among them; [`Splitter`](/reference#splitter) lists its props.\n\nOn a call of your own component, the drag, the drop and the gestures apply to the one element it draws. A component whose body is an `if`, a `for` or several elements has no one element for them, and the build says so (`C07`): wrap what it draws in a `Stack` or a `Row`.")

        Callout("A drag has no keyboard of its own. Give what it does a menu item or a command too (`Move left`, `Move to…`), as the platform's accessibility rules ask of every pointer action.", kind: .important)

        Heading("Listening outside the page's elements", id: "listening-outside-the-page-s-elements").h2

        Markdown("A handler on an element hears that element. For the window, write the handler at the top of the page, a component or `app`, outside any element — `resize`, `scroll`, `hashchange`, `popstate`, `online`, `offline`, `message`, `storage`, `visibilitychange`, `blur` and `focus` are heard there ([Shortcuts for an app](#shortcuts-for-an-app)). It listens for as long as its owner shows:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Scrolled(path: "/", title: "Scrolled", description: "How far down the reader is.") {
    state y = 0
    on scroll { y = window.scrollY }
    Heading("Scrolled").h1
    Text("{y}px down")
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("For any other target or event, use an `effect` with a `cleanup`, which removes the listener when the page leaves. An action named without parentheses is the function itself, so you can hand it to the browser:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Selection(path: "/", title: "Selection", description: "What the reader has selected.") {
    state picked = ""
    action track() { picked = document.getSelection().toString() }
    effect {
        document.addEventListener("selectionchange", track)
        cleanup { document.removeEventListener("selectionchange", track) }
    }
    Heading("Selection").h1
    Text("Selected: {picked}")
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("For the viewport's size, the network and the URL, the language already keeps values current — `viewport`, `network`, `query`, `hash` — so no listener is needed ([State](/learn/07-state-and-reactivity#the-browser-as-values)).")

        Heading("Summary", id: "summary").h2

        Table(caption: "Summary") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("You write").header
                    Table.Cell("It does").header
                    Table.Cell("Section").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`on click \{ … \}`") }
                    Table.Cell("runs when the element fires the event")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Handlers](#handlers)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`on keydown(e) \{ … \}`") }
                    Table.Cell("names the event object")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[The event object](#the-event-object)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`on input(debounce: 300.ms) \{ … \}`, `on scroll(e, throttle: 100.ms) \{ … \}`") }
                    Table.Cell("runs once the events stop; at most once a while")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[How often a handler runs](#how-often-a-handler-runs)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`on visible \{ … \}`") }
                    Table.Cell("runs when the element comes into view")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[How often a handler runs](#how-often-a-handler-runs)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`on key(\"Escape\") \{ … \}`") }
                    Table.Cell("answers to one key or combination")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Keys](#keys)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`on key(\"mod+k mod+s\") \{ … \}` on `app`") }
                    Table.Cell("an app-wide shortcut, here a chord")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Shortcuts for an app](#shortcuts-for-an-app)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`keyIs(e, \"mod+s\")`, `keyLabel(\"mod+s\")`") }
                    Table.Cell("test a key event; write a combination for this system")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Shortcuts for an app](#shortcuts-for-an-app)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`event change(v: Number)`, `emit change(n)`") }
                    Table.Cell("a component's own event")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Emitting events from components](#emitting-events-from-components)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Modal(visible: open, title: \"…\")`") }
                    Table.Cell("a dialog opened by state")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Overlays](#overlays-modals-dialogs-toasts-and-menus)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Popover(visible:, anchor:)`, `Menu(visible:, at:)`") }
                    Table.Cell("layers placed at a control or a point")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Layers](#layers-popovers-menus-and-tooltips-above-everything)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`drag: v`, `on receive(d)`, `on dragmove(d)`") }
                    Table.Cell("drag and drop")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Gestures](#gestures-drag-long-press-swipe-and-the-splitter)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`on longpress(p)`, `on swipe(s)`") }
                    Table.Cell("a held press; a sweep")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Gestures](#gestures-drag-long-press-swipe-and-the-splitter)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Splitter(bind: side, min:, max:)`") }
                    Table.Cell("a handle that sizes a region")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Gestures](#gestures-drag-long-press-swipe-and-the-splitter)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`on scroll \{ … \}` outside any element") }
                    Table.Cell("a window event")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Listening outside the page's elements](#listening-outside-the-page-s-elements)") }
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("The [components reference](/reference) lists every event and prop of each built-in.")

        Heading("Common mistakes", id: "common-mistakes").h2

        Markdown("**Naming more values than a DOM event sends.** A DOM event sends one value, the event, so a second name has nothing to hold:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Spot(path: "/", title: "Spot", description: "Where the pointer clicked.") {
    state at = ""
    Heading("Spot").h1
    Button("Click me") { on click(e, x) { at = "{x}" } }
    Text(at)
}"#, title: "wf · draws T22")

        Markdown("Name the event alone and read from it: `on click(e) \{ at = \"\{e.clientX\}\" \}` ([T22](/errors/T22)).\n\n**A misspelled event.** A handler for an event the element never fires never runs, so the compiler warns:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Clicks(path: "/", title: "Clicks", description: "A misspelled event.") {
    state n = 0
    Heading("Clicks").h1
    Button("+1") { on clik { n = n + 1 } }
    Text("{n}")
}"#, title: "wf · draws V09")

        Markdown("Spell the event as the browser does, `on click` ([V09](/errors/V09)).\n\n**A key combination no keyboard sends.** Modifiers have fixed names, and a word that is not one stops the build:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Search(path: "/", title: "Search", description: "A shortcut no keyboard sends.") {
    state q = ""
    Heading("Search").h1
    Input(bind: q, ref: box, placeholder: "Search")
    on key("command+k") { box.focus() }
}"#, title: "wf · draws E117")

        Markdown("Write `cmd+k`, or `mod+k` for ⌘ on a Mac and Ctrl elsewhere ([E117](/errors/E117)).")
    }
}
